<p>On-page SEO is everything on the page itself that tells Google — and your actual customers — what it's about. Getting this right is usually cheaper and faster than it sounds, since it doesn't require new traffic, just better use of the traffic you already have.</p>

<h2>What's included</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Title tag and meta description rewrites</li>
  <li>Heading structure (H1–H3) that actually reflects page content</li>
  <li>Internal linking between related pages</li>
  <li>Keyword placement that reads naturally, never stuffed</li>
  <li>Content gap filling for thin or missing pages</li>
  <li>Readability improvements for real visitors, not just crawlers</li>
</ul>

<p>The goal is content that a real customer finishes reading — because a page that ranks but doesn't convert isn't actually doing its job.</p>
